\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Post-hoc filtering (ph) vs.\ constrained decoding (cd). In zero-shot settings, post-hoc F1 is higher in 5/6 groups (precision-dominated), but constrained decoding preserves higher recall in all 6/6 groups. At reject rate $>$70\% (Mistral DocRED), post-hoc F1 collapses below constrained. Under fine-tuning, rejection rates drop to 0.6--2.0\% and the two strategies are equivalent ($\Delta \leq 0.55$\,pp; each wins two groups). F1 $\times $100. $^\ddagger $Uses 4096-context LoRA variant; R-F1 differs from Table~\ref {tab:composability}'s standard LoRA configuration.}}{22}{table.caption.69}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:posthoc-extended}{{12}{22}{Post-hoc filtering (ph) vs.\ constrained decoding (cd). In zero-shot settings, post-hoc F1 is higher in 5/6 groups (precision-dominated), but constrained decoding preserves higher recall in all 6/6 groups. At reject rate $>$70\% (Mistral DocRED), post-hoc F1 collapses below constrained. Under fine-tuning, rejection rates drop to 0.6--2.0\% and the two strategies are equivalent ($\Delta \leq 0.55$\,pp; each wins two groups). F1 $\times $100. $^\ddagger $Uses 4096-context LoRA variant; R-F1 differs from Table~\ref {tab:composability}'s standard LoRA configuration}{table.caption.69}{}}
